[18:59:40] ConvaiGRPCLog : Log : Received Text That's a great question, and it gets to the heart of the matter. While we all live in the same world, the way we interact with it can be subtly different depending on our dominant hand.

Imagine a left-handed person using a right-handed computer mouse all day, or consistently having to adjust to tools designed for right-handers. These small, repeated adjustments, while not overtly strenuous, could lead to slightly different muscle development or postural habits over a lifetime.

My point is, while the difference might be imperceptible in daily life, could these constant, tiny adaptations for left-handed individuals mean their respiratory system is working just a fraction harder, or perhaps a fraction less efficiently, than someone who rarely has to make such adjustments? It's a subtle distinction, but one that some researchers have explored.: | Character ID : ac032e3a-931c-11f0-8bf1-42010a7be01f | Session ID : f932f7f16f0a52333bc2e2f5cbd79a19 | ReceivedFinalResponse : False
